  "system_description": {
    "narrative_title": "Monetary-policy shockwaves: bond-market stress and yen intervention",
    "narrative_summary": "Central bank decisions and market reactions — notably the Federal Reserve's 'hold' combined with dissent and long-end bond stress, and the BOJ's stance that left the yen exposed — are driving acute volatility across global fixed-income and FX markets, prompting historic Treasury/BOJ intervention in the yen. As an open system this narrative links stocks (inflation expectations, long-term yields, FX reserves) and flows (rate decisions, bond selling, capital movements, official FX intervention) through reinforcing and balancing feedbacks: higher yields undermine central-bank credibility and elevate borrowing costs, which in turn alters risk appetite and exchange-rate pressures. The system takes inputs from data and geopolitics and emits outputs in the form of market volatility, shifting funding costs and policy responses over short-to-medium horizons.",
    "scope": "US and Japanese monetary policy actions and communications (Fed, BOJ), US Treasury intervention decisions, global long-term bond markets, USD/JPY exchange dynamics, inflation indicators, market participants (investors, dealers) and market infrastructure.",
    "super_system": "Global macro-financial system / world economy",
    "adjacent_subsystems": [
      "Global fixed-income markets (sovereign and corporate bonds)",
      "Foreign-exchange and currency intervention mechanisms",
      "International trade and supply chains (notably Japan's auto and chip sectors)",
      "Geopolitical risk and commodity-price shocks"
    ],
    "evidence_headlines": [
      "The Fed Holds and the Long End Gets Nervous",
      "Fed Holds Rates Steady but Three Officials Vote for Increase",
      "The bond market isn’t buying what Fed Chair Warsh is selling",
      "Japan intervenes to prop up yen ahead of BOJ policy decision, source says",
      "US Treasury intervenes to support yen after Japan steps in, FT reports",
      "US Treasury informed banks that it may intervene in yen, source says"
    ]
